The recursive version tries each possible next index, but the same index can be reached through several paths. A small memo array avoids solving that index again.

`state[i]` stores whether index `i` can eventually reach the end. The last index is an immediate success, and a position with no jump is a failure unless it is already the last position.

```python
class Solution:
    def canJump(self, nums):
        n = len(nums)
        if n == 0:
            return False

        state = [None] * n
        state[n - 1] = True

        def can_reach(index):
            if state[index] is not None:
                return state[index]

            last_next = min(n - 1, index + nums[index])
            for next_index in range(index + 1, last_next + 1):
                if can_reach(next_index):
                    state[index] = True
                    return True

            state[index] = False
            return False

        return can_reach(0)
```

Dry run for `[3,2,1,0,4]`: the search eventually checks index 3, where the jump is zero, so index 3 is marked false. Earlier positions cannot reach index 4 without passing through that blocked part, and the final result is `False`.

The worst-case time is `O(n^2)`, since one index may check many next positions. The memo array and recursion use `O(n)` space.

Starting from the end makes the check fairly direct. `goal` is the first index that must be reached. Moving right to left, an index becomes the new goal when its jump can reach the current one.

This also handles zeros naturally. A zero cannot move the goal by itself, but an earlier position may still jump over it.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean canJump(int[] nums) {
        int goal = nums.length - 1;

        for (int i = nums.length - 2; i >= 0; i--) {
            if (i + nums[i] >= goal) {
                goal = i;
            }
        }

        return goal == 0;
    }
}
```

Runs in `O(n)` time and uses `O(1)` extra space.

The usual input has at least one value. With one value, `goal` starts at zero and the method correctly returns `true`.

I kept a boundary for the farthest index that is reachable. Before using `nums[i]`, I check whether index `i` is already inside that boundary. If it is not, there is a gap that no jump can cross.

Dry run with `nums = [2,3,1,1,4]`:
- Index 0 reaches index 2.
- Index 1 is reachable and extends the boundary to 4.
- The last index is covered, so the result is `true`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool canJump(vector<int>& nums) {
        int farthest = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); i++) {
            if (i > farthest) {
                return false;
            }

            farthest = max(farthest, i + nums[i]);
        }

        return true;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because the array is scanned once.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The input array is not changed.

A zero is only a problem when no earlier reachable position can jump past it. This solution scans from right to left and keeps the distance to the nearest blocking zero. A value larger than that distance can cross the barrier and clears it.

```csharp
public class Solution
{
    public bool CanJump(int[] nums)
    {
        int blockingDistance = 0;

        for (int i = nums.Length - 2; i >= 0; i--)
        {
            if (blockingDistance > 0)
            {
                if (nums[i] > blockingDistance)
                {
                    blockingDistance = 0;
                }
                else
                {
                    blockingDistance++;
                }
            }
            else if (nums[i] == 0)
            {
                blockingDistance = 1;
            }
        }

        return blockingDistance == 0;
    }
}
```

For `[3,2,1,0,4]`, the zero near the end creates a barrier. The values before it can only reach that zero, not pass it, so the barrier reaches the beginning and the method returns `false`.

An array with no zero barrier returns `true`. A single-element array also returns `true` because the loop does not create any barrier.

The scan takes `O(n)` time and `O(1)` extra space. It does not modify `nums`.

This version marks every index that can be reached from the start. For each reachable index, it marks all positions covered by its jump. It is not as compact as the greedy versions, but the reachable states are easy to see while debugging.

```javascript
class Solution {
    canJump(nums) {
        const n = nums.length;
        if (n === 0) {
            return false;
        }

        const reachable = new Array(n).fill(false);
        reachable[0] = true;

        for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (!reachable[i]) {
                continue;
            }

            const last = Math.min(n - 1, i + nums[i]);
            for (let next = i + 1; next <= last; next++) {
                reachable[next] = true;
            }
        }

        return reachable[n - 1];
    }
}
```

For `nums = [2,3,1,1,4]`, index 0 marks 1 and 2. Index 1 then marks through index 4, so the last position becomes reachable.

An empty array returns `false`, while a one-element array returns `true` because the start is already the destination.

Time complexity is `O(n^2)` in the worst case. Space complexity is `O(n)` for the Boolean array. The input is left unchanged.

